# Deliberate binary breaks in 1.15.0, relative to 1.14.0. See the release note in README.md.
#
# THESE FILTERS DO NOT RETIRE. sbt-mima reads every *.backwards.excludes in this directory
# whatever it is named, so this file stays in force until somebody deletes it. Delete it at the
# 1.15.0 post-release, when previousRelease moves to 1.15.0.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# #171: the algorithm objects stop being the entry point.
#
# Every break below is one change. `Kruskal`, `Boruvka`, `BellmanFord`, `AcyclicShortestPaths`
# and `Kosaraju` were top-level public objects, each named for an ALGORITHM and holding a method
# named for the PROBLEM -- `Kruskal.mst`, `BellmanFord.shortestPaths`. Meanwhile `MST.prim` and
# `ShortestPaths.dijkstra` did it the other way round, so the same problem was named in opposite
# directions and a caller who found one learned nothing about the others.
#
# They are now `private[traverse]` implementation homes, reached through the problem objects:
#
#     Kruskal.mst                          ->  MST.kruskal
#     Boruvka.mst                          ->  MST.boruvka
#     BellmanFord.shortestPaths            ->  ShortestPaths.bellmanFord
#     AcyclicShortestPaths.shortestPaths   ->  ShortestPaths.acyclic
#     AcyclicShortestPaths.longestPaths    ->  ShortestPaths.longestAcyclic
#     Kosaraju.stronglyConnectedComponents ->  StronglyConnectedComponents.kosaraju
#
# Source-incompatible as well as binary-incompatible: a caller needs an edit, not a recompile.
# The Java facade is unaffected -- it already had this shape, which is the strongest argument
# that the shape is right.
#
# MiMa reports each object twice, as a class and as an object, because Scala compiles a Scala
# `object` into both.
#
# The problem type is ClassNoLongerCheckedProblem, which is what MiMa calls a definition that has
# left the checked API rather than vanished. Its message -- "is not part of the API in current
# version" -- reads like an access problem, and a filter written from that wording silently
# matches nothing. Read the problem type out of MiMa's own suggestion block rather than inferring
# it from the prose.
#
ProblemFilters.exclude[ClassNoLongerCheckedProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.Kruskal")
ProblemFilters.exclude[ClassNoLongerCheckedProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.Kruskal$")
ProblemFilters.exclude[ClassNoLongerCheckedProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.Boruvka")
ProblemFilters.exclude[ClassNoLongerCheckedProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.Boruvka$")
ProblemFilters.exclude[ClassNoLongerCheckedProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.BellmanFord")
ProblemFilters.exclude[ClassNoLongerCheckedProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.BellmanFord$")
ProblemFilters.exclude[ClassNoLongerCheckedProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.AcyclicShortestPaths")
ProblemFilters.exclude[ClassNoLongerCheckedProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.AcyclicShortestPaths$")
ProblemFilters.exclude[ClassNoLongerCheckedProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.Kosaraju")
ProblemFilters.exclude[ClassNoLongerCheckedProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.Kosaraju$")
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SCCResult moves from Kosaraju.scala to StronglyConnectedComponents.scala.
#
# The type is the result of the PROBLEM, not of one algorithm for it: a second implementation --
# Tarjan's, say -- would return the same thing, and would have had to import it from its rival.
# Scala compiles a file's top-level definitions into a synthetic class named after the file, so
# moving the alias empties `Kosaraju$package` and creates `StronglyConnectedComponents$package`.
#
# Worth recording WHY these two are filtered, because the first reading of them was wrong. They
# appeared while the alias still sat in Kosaraju.scala, which would have meant a public type had
# silently become unreachable -- a real regression wearing the same error message. Moving the
# alias is what makes the report legitimate rather than something to be filtered away.
#
ProblemFilters.exclude[MissingClassProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.Kosaraju$package")
ProblemFilters.exclude[MissingClassProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.Kosaraju$package$")
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The MST algorithms return MST-specific result types.
#
# Prim returned `TraversalResult[V, Edge[V, E]]` and Kruskal/Boruvka returned
# `AbstractEdgeTraversalResult[Edge[V, E]]`. Those have no common supertype that says anything
# useful: the first is keyed by VERTEX and the second by POSITION, so `TraversalResult`'s own key
# parameter is filled differently and no function could take all three results.
#
# They now return `MSTParentMap` and `MSTEdgeList`, which share `MSTResult` -- a trait carrying
# `edges` and `totalWeight`. The representations are deliberately NOT normalised: Prim's parent
# map and Kruskal's ascending-weight order are both real information, and 1.13.0 separated the
# two branches for that reason. What is shared is a VIEW, as `AbstractComponentResult` does for
# the component algorithms.
#
# Source-compatible for existing callers: MSTEdgeList is still an AbstractEdgeTraversalResult and
# so still a Seq, and MSTParentMap is still an AbstractVertexTraversalResult. Every operation that
# worked before still works; 794 tests needed no edit.
#
# The two `mst` entries are the private[traverse] implementation objects, whose return type
# changed before their visibility did -- the ClassNoLongerCheckedProblem filters above cover the
# visibility, not the signature.
#
ProblemFilters.exclude[IncompatibleResultTypeProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.Kruskal.mst(com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.adjunct.UndirectedGraph,scala.math.Ordering)com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.AbstractEdgeTraversalResult")
ProblemFilters.exclude[IncompatibleResultTypeProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.Boruvka.mst(com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.adjunct.UndirectedGraph,cats.kernel.Monoid,scala.math.Ordering)com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.AbstractEdgeTraversalResult")
ProblemFilters.exclude[IncompatibleResultTypeProblem]("com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.MST.prim(com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.adjunct.UndirectedGraph,java.lang.Object,cats.kernel.Monoid,scala.math.Ordering,scala.util.Random)com.phasmidsoftware.gryphon.traverse.TraversalResult")
